= Crowde and DuFour

With Crowde’s return from vacation, many of his loyalists—and [+ royalist_leader +] himself—interpret his prolonged silence as quiet scheming. Frustration is beginning to boil over among his supporters, who have grown impatient with his inaction and are now publicly criticizing the party's all-or-nothing strategy.

[+ royalist_leader +]'s suspicions are confirmed when Staal purposefully leaks news of a private meeting between Crowde and President DuFour. The President is said to have expressed relief at finding “nothing radical” about Crowde and signaled willingness to appoint him as vice chancellor. Staal, however, prefers to keep him outside the cabinet—believing that such an appointment would trigger an irreparable rift between Crowde and the party over his betrayal.

Still, most of Crowde’s followers aren’t seeking a break. They hope for reconciliation, blaming Goebbels and Gorrick—his chief rivals—for leading Gallax down a ruinous path. Staal appears to share that hope, intending to use Crowde as a lever to shift [+ royalist_leader +] once the two reconciled.

> *"Crowde is plotting. He was with DuFour... That’s what I call a traitor. I always saw through him."*

 - Joseph Goebbels, to his diary, [+ year_display +]

 